[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Its own table disagrees: the
reports merged into it have more than one cause, and only one of them has a ticket. This page
splits #4471 by what each report shows, and gives every problem its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes only the parser row. The other rows stay broken after it ships.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable
  ticket={4471}
  tickets={{
    parser: 'https://github.com/RE093/kb-4471/issues/1',
    hint: 'https://github.com/RE093/kb-4471/issues/2',
    'one-day': 'https://github.com/RE093/kb-4471/issues/3',
  }}
/>

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first, and every tenant on file
  writes the day first. Day and month swap.
- **`hint`**: booking-v3's form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, then reads the
  input day first. People type what the hint says. A parser fix does not touch it.
- **`one-day`**: the date moves by one day, not by a swapped month, and one meeting landed on
  different days for people in different time zones. The desk already noted these
  (`desk-notes.json`); the old table still filed them under `parser` by tenant.

## How the rows are counted

The tenant decides the cause, not the intake tag: intake guesses from the reporter's browser,
and its tags disagree with the cause in both directions (the "tagged as" column). A report the
desk read as a one-day move is counted there, whatever its tenant. The export repeats some
reports across overlapping pages; each id counts once.

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject line alone, and every
row above has the same kind of subject: "wrong day", "date is wrong". Matching subjects say
two reports sound alike, not that they share a cause. Its consequence, "engineering merges them
later", ran the other way here: different causes ended up in one ticket and never came apart.

## Lessons

- Before marking a big ticket known, check that one fix closes every report in it.
- Count by cause, not by subject or intake tag.

